course: biology
lesson: 190
title: Strange discoveries: living fossils, hidden apes and animals found in drawers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You might think every animal on Earth has already been found, but scientists describe thousands of new kinds every year, mostly insects and small sea animals, and now and then something big.
explain: Some discoveries are animals known only as fossils that turn out to be alive, like the coelacanth fish caught in 1938 or the Wollemi pine found in a hidden canyon near Sydney in 1994.
explain: Others were hiding in plain sight: the olinguito was found by a scientist looking at old skins in a museum drawer, like finding a new toy at the bottom of the toy box you thought you knew.
explain: Often local people already knew the animal; «discovered» means scientists described and named it, and many animals may die out before anyone gets the chance.
fact: In 1938 a coelacanth was caught off South Africa, though scientists thought coelacanths had died out with the dinosaurs, and a second kind was found at an Indonesian fish market in 1997.
fact: The saola, a rare deer-like animal from remote mountain forests of Vietnam and Laos, was discovered in 1992 and has never been seen by a scientist in the wild.
fact: The Wollemi pine, known only from fossils, was found in 1994 in a canyon near Sydney; fewer than a hundred grow in the wild, and their place is kept secret.
fact: The olinguito of the Andes cloud forests was named in 2013 after scientists studied museum skins, the first new meat-eating mammal found in the Americas in over thirty years.
fact: The Lord Howe Island stick insect was thought extinct for eighty years until a few were found in 2001 on a tall sea rock near the island, and zoos have since bred thousands.
fact: The megamouth shark, which lives deep and eats plankton (tiny animals and plants drifting in the sea), was discovered in 1976 off Hawaii, and fewer than three hundred have ever been seen.
fact: People once thought giant squids were sea monsters, and the kraken stories may have come from them, but they are real deep-sea animals.
fact: Scientists describe thousands of new kinds of animals every year, mostly insects and small sea animals, and many animals may go extinct before being found.
fact: Mountain gorillas were known to local people long before scientists first described them in 1902, and now there are about a thousand.
fact: Bone-eating worms, discovered on a whale skeleton in 2002, have no mouth or stomach, use roots to eat bones, and help break down whale skeletons.
fact: In 2017 the Skywalker hoolock gibbon of China and Myanmar was named as a new species after scientists compared its fur, teeth and songs, and fewer than about two hundred are thought to live in the wild.
exercise: Why was the coelacanth caught in 1938 such a surprise? | Scientists thought it had died out with the dinosaurs | It could talk | It was caught in a desert | It had little legs and walked about on the land at night
exercise: Where was the second kind of coelacanth found in 1997? | At a fish market in Indonesia | In a river in the far north of Canada | In a zoo in London | On a beach in Iceland
exercise: When was the saola discovered? | In 1992 | In 1792 | In 1892 | It has never been found
exercise: What is strange about the saola even today? | It has never been seen by a scientist in the wild | It lives in every city park, where children feed it bread | It is the most common deer | It lives only in zoos
exercise: How was the Wollemi pine known before 1994? | Only from fossils | From every garden in Sydney | From old paintings | From a children's book
exercise: Why is the place where Wollemi pines grow kept secret? | Fewer than a hundred grow in the wild and they need protecting | It is haunted | Nobody remembers where it is, so it cannot be visited at all any more | It is under the sea
exercise: How was the olinguito discovered? | Scientists studied skins in a museum | A child found it hiding in a school bag | It walked into a city | It was seen on the Moon
exercise: Where was the lost Lord Howe Island stick insect found in 2001? | On a tall sea rock near the island | In a supermarket | Under the thick sea ice of Antarctica | In a museum drawer
exercise: What have zoos done for the Lord Howe Island stick insect? | Bred thousands of them | Turned them into pets for everyone | Sent them to space | Nothing at all
exercise: What does the megamouth shark eat? | Plankton | Small boats | Seals and penguins | Reef coral
exercise: How many megamouth sharks have ever been seen? | Fewer than three hundred | Millions, all over Australia | Exactly one | None at all
exercise: Where may the kraken stories have come from? | Giant squids | Whales singing | Giant sea crabs | Floating logs
exercise: Which animals make up most of the new kinds found every year? | Insects and small sea animals | Elephants and other big animals | Big cats such as wild leopards | Great whales of the deep ocean
exercise: Who knew about mountain gorillas before 1902? | Local people | Nobody at all | Only astronauts | Only Charles Darwin
exercise: How do bone-eating worms eat whale bones without a mouth? | They use roots to eat into the bones | They suck through a straw | They bite with sharp teeth | They wait for the bones to melt in the warm water
exercise: How did scientists know the Skywalker gibbon was a new species? | Its fur, teeth and songs were different from other hoolock gibbons | It looked like a film hero | It had a light sabre | It spoke a new language that no other gibbon in the forest could understand
exercise: Why is the Skywalker gibbon in danger? | Fewer than about two hundred are thought to live in the wild | It lives on another planet | It is too fast to catch | There are millions of them, but they hide in the trees all day
test: «a giant squid washed up, it's the kraken! run!» What is right? | It is a real deep-sea animal; kraken stories may come from squids like it | It is a sea monster that sinks ships | It is a giant octopus with 20 arms | It is a whale's tail
test: «giant squid: 8 arms and 2 long tentacles, we found 3 on the beach in a story, how many arms and tentacles» | 30 | 24 | 10 | 16
test: «2 saolas, each with 2 long horns and 4 legs, how many horns» | 4 | 2 | 8 | 12
test: «they named a gibbon after Luke Skywalker? are u kidding» What is right? | Yes, in 2017 the Skywalker hoolock gibbon was named as a new species | No, gibbons cannot have names | It was named after a footballer | It was a robot from the film
test: In King Kong a giant ape is found on a hidden island. What is true about real hidden apes? | Mountain gorillas were described by scientists only in 1902, though local people knew them | Giant apes the size of buildings live on islands | All apes were found a thousand years ago | Gorillas live only in films
test: «scientists already found every animal, nothing left» What is right? | Thousands of new kinds are described every year | Right, nothing new is found | Only about one new animal is found in each century | New animals are made in labs
test: What would happen to an animal that dies out before anyone finds it? | Scientists would never even know it existed | It would be found anyway in a zoo | It would come back to life | Nothing, because all the animals are already known
test: «found a new animal in a museum drawer? ha, sure» What is right? | The olinguito was named in 2013 after scientists studied museum skins | Museums only keep paintings | Drawers cannot hold animals | It was a toy
test: «a fisherman caught a fish that died with the dinosaurs!» What is right? | The coelacanth was thought extinct but was caught alive in 1938 | It was a dinosaur | It was a stone fossil, not a fish, dug out of the rocks by the sea | It was a shark in disguise
test: «stick insects on 1 rock, thought they were extinct, now zoos have thousands» What happened? | A few were found in 2001 and bred in zoos | Scientists made them from fossils | They flew back by themselves from another island | They were robots
test: In Jurassic Park, dinosaurs are brought back. Which real plant from dinosaur times was found alive in 1994? | The Wollemi pine near Sydney | The sunflower of the prairies | The wild dog rose of English hedges | The oak trees of an English forest
exam: Identify the circumstance which rendered the capture of a coelacanth in 1938 remarkable. | The group was believed to have become extinct at the time of the dinosaurs | It was the largest fish ever caught | It was taken from a freshwater lake in a desert | It was found to breathe air exclusively
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the discovery of the olinguito? | It was identified in 2013 through the examination of skins preserved in a museum | It was observed walking in a European capital | It was discovered in 1902 by explorers in Africa | It was produced by breeding in captivity
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the term discovery as applied to animals known beforehand to local inhabitants. | That scientists described and named the animal for the first time | That the animal came into existence at that moment and not before | That no person had ever seen it | That it was brought from another planet
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the bone-eating worms. | They lack mouth and stomach and consume whale bones by means of roots | They inhabit desert sand and consume stones | They possess sharp teeth for gnawing bones | They were discovered in the year 1802
